# PART 3 — CRITICAL: FIX THE EDITOR STATE-SYNCHRONIZATION BUG

Investigate this exact pattern in `LectureNotesEditor.tsx`:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  setBlocks(markdownToBlocks(initialContent));
}, [initialContent]);
```

The parent `LectureNotesWorkspace` updates the active note content when the editor calls `onChange`.

This means every keystroke can cause:

```text
User types
    ↓
Editor serializes blocks
    ↓
Parent updates activeNote.content
    ↓
initialContent changes
    ↓
Editor reparses all Markdown
    ↓
All block IDs can be regenerated
    ↓
React replaces editor inputs
    ↓
Focus and cursor can be lost
```

This is a probable root cause of the reported typing difficulty.

**Fix the state architecture. Do not merely add more focus calls.**

## Required behavior

- Typing must not remount the active block.
- Cursor position must remain stable.
- Text selection must remain stable.
- Table-cell focus must remain stable.
- List-item focus must remain stable.
- Math and educational block state must remain stable.
- Stable block identifiers must be preserved.
- The editor must not reparse its entire document after every local edit.

Separate:

1. Initial document loading.
2. Switching to another note.
3. Local editing.
4. Parent persistence updates.
5. Genuine external document updates.

Use an explicit note identity and an appropriate synchronization strategy.

Use refs or another robust document synchronization mechanism to distinguish locally generated changes from genuine external updates.

Do not reset the document whenever the parent sends back the content produced by the editor itself.

Ensure that switching from Note A to Note B correctly loads Note B.

Ensure that a remote update does not silently overwrite unsaved local changes.

Preserve unsaved edits safely during synchronization.

