# PART 5 — FIX SLASH COMMANDS

The current slash-command implementation opens a fixed full-screen modal and moves focus to an unrelated search input.

This makes writing awkward.

Rework the slash-command interaction.

## Expected behavior

When the user types `/` in an editable paragraph:

1. Detect the slash at the current cursor position.
2. Open a compact contextual command menu near the cursor.
3. Let the user continue typing to search commands.
4. Filter commands using the text following `/`.
5. Preserve the current document and caret context.
6. Allow keyboard and mouse selection.
7. Insert the selected block at the correct location.
8. Remove the slash command text from the document.
9. Focus the new block or restore the original cursor appropriately.

Example:

```text
User types:
/math

Command menu filters to mathematical commands.

User chooses Equation.

Equation block is inserted at the cursor position.

Editor focuses the appropriate next editing position.
```

## Keyboard requirements

- Arrow Up/Down navigates commands.
- Enter selects a command.
- Escape closes the menu.
- Typing filters options.
- Backspace updates the search query correctly.
- Selecting an action must not duplicate the slash text.
- Closing the menu should not destroy document focus.

Do not use a full-screen dimmed modal for normal slash-command insertion.

A floating contextual menu is preferred.

Make it responsive and usable on mobile.

